  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Using over 245 million vehicle registration records, this study quantifies fuel-cycle carbon transfers from plug-in electric vehicles (PEVs) across 285 Chinese cities. In 2020, economically developed cities outsourced 41.8% of their PEV-related emissions to less developed cities, causing per-PEV emissions in the receiving cities to exceed those of conventional vehicles by 16.9% to 52.0%. Projections show this intercity emission inequality will remain high through 2030 due to widening gaps in vehicle ownership, only declining as the national power grid decarbonizes.",
      "why": "The study reveals a spatial mismatch where the decarbonization benefits of electric vehicles in wealthy cities are achieved by shifting the carbon and economic mitigation burdens to poorer, power-producing municipalities."
    }
  },
